    "question": "A simple graph G has a vertex of degree 5 and all other vertices have degree 2. What is the minimum possible number of vertices in G?",
    "options": {
      "A": "4",
      "B": "5",
      "C": "6",
      "D": "7"
    },
    "correct_answer": "D",
    "explanation": "The vertex of degree 5 must be adjacent to 5 distinct vertices, each of which already uses one of its degree‑2 edges. To give each of those vertices a second edge, they must be paired, requiring at least 6 additional vertices, so the total minimum is 1 + 5 + 1 = 7.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
